\subsection{Weekly teaching: A cron report of ungraded work}
\label{sec:cron-ungraded}

The previous script answers the question \enquote{what needs grading?} when
we sit down and ask it.
A teacher with several courses also wants the question asked for them: a
mail every Sunday evening listing what is still waiting, course by course,
so that nothing is forgotten in a course one does not look at every day.
That is a job for [[cron]], and running unattended changes what the script
must do.
\begin{description}
\item[What to report lives in a file] Nobody types a course pattern on the
  command line of a cron job, and the patterns are personal: which courses
  one teaches, which assignment groups are graded by hand, which
  resubmissions can be ignored.
\item[The report must survive the turn of the year] Course rounds are
  named by year, such as [[prgi25]] and [[prgi26]], and a cron job that
  names this year's round silently reports nothing once the next round
  starts.
\item[The output is read in a mail] Each part of the report needs a
  header, so that the reader can tell which course a row belongs to, and
  which course had nothing to grade.
\end{description}

This script, unlike the others in this chapter, is shipped with
[[canvaslms]]: it is installed as [[canvaslms/scripts/ungraded.sh]] in the
package's directory, so the script on a server always matches the
[[canvaslms]] installed there.
That is the reason the course list is kept out of the script.
A version with one's own courses written into it must be edited, and kept
somewhere, by hand, and every upgrade of [[canvaslms]] would overwrite it.
The script is generic and the file is personal, so an upgrade replaces
only the script.

The whole script is four steps:
<<[[ungraded.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
# ungraded.sh - report ungraded submissions, one section per config line
#
# Usage: ungraded.sh [config-file]
#
# Each line of the config file is
#   course regex <TAB> assignment group regex <TAB> grades to skip
# {YY} in the course regex matches last year's and this year's rounds.

config="${1:-${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/canvaslms/ungraded.tsv}"
<<refuse to run without a readable config>>
<<compute the year window [[years]]>>
<<report each line of the config>>
@ The default location is beside [[canvaslms]]'s own configuration
file, so that the two are found, and backed up, together.
An argument overrides it, which is how one keeps several reports, for
instance one for oneself and one for a co-teacher.


\subsubsection{The configuration file}

Each line of the file describes one section of the report.
It has up to three columns, separated by tabs, the same separator that
[[canvaslms]] itself prints:
\begin{description}
\item[Course] A regex matching the course, as given to [[-c]].
  The placeholder [[{YY}]] stands for this year's and last year's
  two-digit years.
\item[Assignment group] A regex matching assignment groups, as given to
  [[-A]].
  If it is left out, all groups are reported.
\item[Grades to skip] An optional regex.
  A row whose current grade matches it is left out of the report.
\end{description}
Empty lines and lines starting with [[#]] are ignored, so the file can
explain itself.

\Cref{tab:ungraded-config} shows the file that produces the weekly report
for the courses of this manual's author.
The first four lines ask for the hand-graded assignment groups of two
courses.
The last line needs its third column because of how
[[submissions list -U]] counts: a student who resubmits after passing has
an ungraded submission again, but in that course a passed lab is never
regraded, so rows whose current grade is already passing ([[A]]--[[E]] or
[[P]]) are only noise.

\begin{table}
\centering
\caption{A configuration file for the weekly report.
  The columns are separated by single tabs.}
\label{tab:ungraded-config}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Course & Assignment group & Grades to skip \\
\midrule
[[vetcyb{YY}]] & [[^INL1]] & \\
[[vetcyb{YY}]] & [[^Participation]] & \\
[[tilkry{YY}]] & [[^INL1]] & \\
[[tilkry{YY}]] & [[LAB1]] & \\
[[prg[im]{YY}]] & [[LAB[123]]] & [[^[A-EP]]] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsubsection{Failing loudly without a configuration}

A cron job that silently prints nothing looks exactly like a week with
nothing to grade.
So a missing or unreadable file is an error: [[cron]] mails whatever the
job writes to standard error, and the message names the file it looked for
and reminds the reader of its format.
<<refuse to run without a readable config>>=
if [ ! -r "$config" ]; then
  echo "${0##*/}: cannot read configuration file $config" >&2
  echo "Each line: course<TAB>assignment group<TAB>grades to skip" >&2
  exit 1
fi
@


\subsubsection{The year window}

The [[{YY}]] placeholder becomes a regex that matches two rounds, such as
[[(25|26)]].
Both are needed in the autumn: the new round has started, but last year's
students are still handing in late work.
Matching the current year alone would make the report forget those
students on New Year's Eve; matching any year would bring back every
student who never finished.

Two details make the computation correct for every year rather than for
most.
The year is read as a base-10 number ([[10#]]), since the shell would
otherwise take [[08]] and [[09]] as broken octal numbers.
And the previous year wraps around and is padded to two digits, so that
2000 gives [[(99|00)]] and 2010 gives [[(09|10)]], not [[(-1|00)]] or
[[(9|10)]].
<<compute the year window [[years]]>>=
this_year=$(date +%y)
last_year=$(printf %02d $(( (10#$this_year + 99) % 100 )))
years="(${last_year}|${this_year})"
@


\subsubsection{One section per line}

The main loop reads the file one line at a time and turns each line into
one call of [[canvaslms submissions list -U]], under a header that shows
the patterns actually used.
<<report each line of the config>>=
while IFS=$'\t' read -r course group skip <&3 || [ -n "$course" ]; do
  case "$course" in
    ""|"#"*) continue ;;
  esac
  course="${course//\{YY\}/$years}"
  group="${group:-.*}"
  printf '### %s\t%s\n' "$course" "$group"
  canvaslms submissions list -c "$course" -A "$group" -U \
    | <<drop rows whose grade matches [[skip]]>>
  echo
done 3< "$config"
@

Three things in the loop are less obvious than they look.

The file is read on file descriptor~3 instead of standard input.
Every command in a [[while read]] loop inherits the loop's standard input,
so if the file were on standard input, any command in the body that reads
it would consume the rest of the configuration, and the report would end
after the first section without an error.
[[canvaslms]] does not read standard input today, but [[ssh]], for
example, does, and a script that runs unattended should not depend on
that.

The test after [[read]] keeps the last line when the file does not end in
a newline.
In that case [[read]] fills in the variables but reports failure, and
without the test the last section of a file saved by some editors would be
silently dropped.

[[canvaslms]] exits with an error when a section has nothing to grade, and
the message, such as [[No submissions found matching the criteria]], ends
up in the mail under the section's header.
We let it through rather than pass [[-q]].
The same message is what one sees when a pattern in the file is mistyped
and matches no course at all, and a report that hides that would never be
corrected.


\subsubsection{Skipping rows by grade}

The third column is matched against the grade column only.
The script this one replaces used
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms submissions -c "prg[im]${POTENTIAL_YEAR}" -A "LAB[123]" -U \
  | grep -Ev "\s[A-EP]"
\end{minted}
which looks for a whitespace character followed by a passing grade
\emph{anywhere} in the row.
The student's name is also preceded by a tab, so every student whose name
begins with a letter from A to E, or with P, disappeared from the report,
whatever their grade.

[[submissions list]] prints the course, the assignment, the student, the
grade and two dates, so the grade is field~4 of a tab-separated row, and
[[awk]] can test that field alone.
An empty third column keeps every row.
<<drop rows whose grade matches [[skip]]>>=
skip="$skip" awk -F'\t' 'ENVIRON["skip"] == "" || $4 !~ ENVIRON["skip"]'
@ The regex is passed through the environment rather than with
[[awk -v]], because [[-v]] interprets backslash escapes in the value: a
pattern such as [[\.]] would reach [[awk]] as [[.]].


\subsubsection{Running it from cron}

The script needs only [[bash]], [[awk]] and [[canvaslms]].
Its location depends on how [[canvaslms]] was installed; Python can tell:
\begin{minted}{bash}
python3 -c 'import canvaslms, pathlib
print(pathlib.Path(canvaslms.__file__).parent / "scripts" / "ungraded.sh")'
\end{minted}
With [[pipx]], the [[python3]] to ask is the one in the package's own
environment, typically [[~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/canvaslms/bin/python]].
One can either point the crontab at that path, or copy the script, for
example to [[~/ungraded.sh]], whenever [[canvaslms]] is upgraded.
A crontab entry that mails the report every Sunday at 23.45 is then
\begin{minted}{text}
45 23 * * Sun ${HOME}/ungraded.sh
\end{minted}

The script leaves the credentials to [[canvaslms]], and that needs care
under [[cron]].
Jobs run with an almost empty environment and outside the desktop session
in which the keyring is usually unlocked, so the token stored by
[[canvaslms login]] may not be reachable.
The robust choice is the environment variables [[CANVAS_SERVER]] and
[[CANVAS_TOKEN]] (\cref{sec:environment-variables}), set by the crontab
itself or by a file that [[BASH_ENV]] names.
The script deliberately does not read a profile of its own: what a job's
environment contains is the job's business, not the report's.


\subsubsection{Verifying the script}

We test the script by running it, with the two programs whose answers
change from run to run replaced.
A stub [[canvaslms]] early on [[PATH]] records the arguments it was called
with and prints rows the test chose, so the tests need no Canvas
connection and no personal data.
A stub [[date]] fixes the year, because a test that computes the expected
year from the real clock would fail when the clock crosses New Year
between the script's call and the test's.
Everything else, [[bash]], [[awk]] and the script itself, is real.
<<test [[ungraded.py]]>>=
"""Tests for the cron report script ungraded.sh."""

import argparse
import json
import os
import pathlib
import subprocess
import sys

import pytest

import canvaslms
import canvaslms.cli.submissions

SCRIPT = pathlib.Path(canvaslms.__file__).parent / "scripts" / "ungraded.sh"

<<stubs for [[canvaslms]] and [[date]]>>
<<test functions>>
@

The [[canvaslms]] stub appends its arguments as one JSON line to a log and
prints the rows in [[rows.tsv]].
It also reads its standard input to the end, as a misbehaving command
might, which is how the tests catch a loop that gives its configuration
to the commands in its body.
The [[run]] fixture writes the configuration, runs the script with a
given year and returns the result together with the recorded calls.
<<stubs for [[canvaslms]] and [[date]]>>=
CANVASLMS_STUB = """#!{python}
import json, sys
sys.stdin.read()
with open({log!r}, "a") as log:
  log.write(json.dumps(sys.argv[1:]) + "\\n")
with open({rows!r}) as rows:
  sys.stdout.write(rows.read())
"""

DATE_STUB = """#!/bin/sh
echo {year}
"""

@pytest.fixture
def run(tmp_path):
  """Return a function that runs the script on a config and stubbed rows."""
  bin_dir = tmp_path / "bin"
  bin_dir.mkdir()
  log = tmp_path / "calls.jsonl"
  rows = tmp_path / "rows.tsv"

  def run_script(config, *, rows_text="", year="26", args=None, env=None):
    (bin_dir / "canvaslms").write_text(CANVASLMS_STUB.format(
      python=sys.executable, log=str(log), rows=str(rows)))
    (bin_dir / "date").write_text(DATE_STUB.format(year=year))
    for stub in bin_dir.iterdir():
      stub.chmod(0o755)
    rows.write_text(rows_text)
    config_file = tmp_path / "ungraded.tsv"
    if config is not None:
      config_file.write_text(config)
    run_env = dict(os.environ)
    run_env["PATH"] = f"{bin_dir}{os.pathsep}{run_env['PATH']}"
    run_env.update(env or {})
    result = subprocess.run(
      ["bash", str(SCRIPT)] + (args if args is not None
                               else [str(config_file)]),
      env=run_env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
      capture_output=True, text=True)
    calls = ([json.loads(line) for line in log.read_text().splitlines()]
             if log.exists() else [])
    if log.exists():
      log.unlink()
    return result, calls

  return run_script
@

First, the script must at least be valid [[bash]], and must refuse to run
without its configuration, naming the file it looked for.
<<test functions>>=
def test_script_is_valid_bash():
  subprocess.run(["bash", "-n", str(SCRIPT)], check=True)

def test_missing_config_is_an_error(run, tmp_path):
  missing = tmp_path / "nowhere.tsv"
  result, calls = run(None, args=[str(missing)])
  assert result.returncode != 0
  assert str(missing) in result.stderr
  assert calls == []
@

Without an argument, the file is looked for beside [[canvaslms]]'s own
configuration.
<<test functions>>=
def test_default_config_is_in_the_config_directory(run, tmp_path):
  config_dir = tmp_path / "xdg" / "canvaslms"
  config_dir.mkdir(parents=True)
  (config_dir / "ungraded.tsv").write_text("dd1301\tLAB\n")
  result, calls = run(None, args=[],
                      env={"XDG_CONFIG_HOME": str(tmp_path / "xdg")})
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert [call[call.index("-c") + 1] for call in calls] == ["dd1301"]
@

The year window must be right for ordinary years and for the years where
octal parsing or the wrap-around would break it, and a pattern without
the placeholder must be passed on unchanged.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("year,window", [
  ("26", "(25|26)"), ("10", "(09|10)"), ("08", "(07|08)"), ("00", "(99|00)"),
])
def test_year_placeholder_matches_two_rounds(run, year, window):
  result, calls = run("prg[im]{YY}\tLAB[123]\n", year=year)
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert calls[0][calls[0].index("-c") + 1] == f"prg[im]{window}"

def test_pattern_without_placeholder_is_unchanged(run):
  result, calls = run("DD1301 HT26\tLAB\n")
  assert calls[0][calls[0].index("-c") + 1] == "DD1301 HT26"
@

Every line, the last one included even without a final newline, gives
exactly one section, while comments and empty lines give none.
Since the stub reads its standard input, these tests also fail if the
loop's input reached the commands in it.
<<test functions>>=
def test_one_section_per_config_line(run):
  config = ("# my courses\n"
            "\n"
            "vetcyb{YY}\t^INL1\n"
            "tilkry{YY}\tLAB1\n"
            "dd1301")
  result, calls = run(config)
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  courses = [call[call.index("-c") + 1] for call in calls]
  assert courses == ["vetcyb(25|26)", "tilkry(25|26)", "dd1301"]
  headers = [line for line in result.stdout.splitlines()
             if line.startswith("### ")]
  assert headers == ["### vetcyb(25|26)\t^INL1",
                     "### tilkry(25|26)\tLAB1",
                     "### dd1301\t.*"]
@

The grade filter is the reason this script exists in its current form, so
we test it on the case the old [[grep]] got wrong: a student whose name
begins with a passing grade's letter, and who has no grade yet.
<<test functions>>=
ROWS = (
  "prgi26\tLAB1\tBeata Exempel\t\t2026-10-01 10:00\t\n"
  "prgi26\tLAB1\tTolvan Tolvansson\tP\t2026-10-02 10:00\t2026-09-20 12:00\n"
  "prgi26\tLAB2\tTolvan Tolvansson\tF\t2026-10-03 10:00\t2026-09-21 12:00\n"
)

def test_skip_column_tests_the_grade_only(run):
  result, _ = run("prg[im]{YY}\tLAB[123]\t^[A-EP]\n", rows_text=ROWS)
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert "Beata Exempel" in result.stdout
  assert "Tolvan Tolvansson\tP" not in result.stdout
  assert "Tolvan Tolvansson\tF" in result.stdout

def test_no_skip_column_keeps_every_row(run):
  result, _ = run("prg[im]{YY}\tLAB[123]\n", rows_text=ROWS)
  assert all(row in result.stdout for row in ROWS.splitlines())
@

Finally, the stub accepts any arguments, so we check the recorded calls
against the real parser of the [[submissions]] command.
If one of its options is renamed, this test fails, not the cron job on a
Sunday evening.
<<test functions>>=
def test_calls_parse_with_the_real_parser(run):
  result, calls = run("vetcyb{YY}\t^INL1\ndd1301\n")
  parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
  canvaslms.cli.submissions.add_command(
    parser.add_subparsers(dest="command"))
  parsed = [parser.parse_args(call) for call in calls]
  assert [args.submissions_subcommand for args in parsed] == ["list"] * 2
  assert all(args.ungraded for args in parsed)
  assert [(args.course, args.assignment_group) for args in parsed] == [
    ("vetcyb(25|26)", "^INL1"), ("dd1301", ".*")]
@
